

BOOT.CONF(5)         UNIX Programmer's Manual        BOOT.CONF(5)



NAME
     boot.conf - stage-2 boot loader configuration

DESCRIPTION
     The file /etc/boot.conf is read by the stage-2 loader before
     the kernel starts.  Blank lines and text from a `#' to the end
     of line are ignored.  Each directive is dispatched on its
     first letter, in either case:

     kernel path
          The kernel image to load, e.g. `kernel /fgxunix'.  Read
          in a first pass over the file; the path may be up to 64
          bytes.

     region bb oooo ssss
          Claim a fixed memory region: bank, offset and size, in
          hexadecimal.

     bank bb
          Claim a whole bank, in hexadecimal.

     Any other directive is SKIPPED rather than rejected.  That is
     deliberate: an older boot.conf carrying a `user-banks' line
     still boots, instead of double-claiming a handle the memory
     manager has already placed.

     Only the first 512 bytes of the file are read.

     THE FILE IS OPTIONAL.  Without it the loader claims nothing
     and loads the kernel from its built-in default path, which is
     what the shipped `kernel /fgxunix' line already names -- so
     deleting the file changes nothing.

     A region or bank claim is rarely right.  Both the kernel and
     the rest of the operating system are placed dynamically by the
     memory manager, so there is no fixed bank to reserve.  Bank-0
     working RAM -- the kernel stacks, the block staging buffer and
     the task stack arena -- is claimed by the loader's own Tool
     Note 27 claim, not from this file, so a `region' covering bank
     0 duplicates a claim already made.

     The parser is src/boot/stage2.s.

FILES
     /etc/boot.conf

SEE ALSO
     init(8), modload.conf(5), fgxunix(5)



